class_name Encyclopedia
extends RefCounted
## The in-game encyclopedia: the real history behind every ship class, aircraft and weapon in
## the game, and the battles of the Pacific war. Written for a player who wants to know what
## they are commanding; game statistics are added by the panel from Data.TYPES.
##
## Facts here are the standard reference figures (Friedman, Lacroix & Wells, Jentschura,
## Morison, Dull). Keep entries plain, accurate and free of glorification.

## Ship classes: type_id -> {real: real-world particulars, text: history, ships: named units}
const SHIPS := {
	# ---------------------------------------------------------------- United States
	"fletcher": {"real": "2,050 tons standard · 376 ft · 36.5 kn · 273 men · 5 x 5-inch/38, 10 x 21-inch torpedo tubes, 40 mm and 20 mm AA · 175 built 1942-44",
		"text": "The Fletchers were the first US destroyers designed after the treaty limits lapsed, and the largest class of destroyers ever built. Flush-decked, with five single 5-inch dual-purpose mounts, two quintuple torpedo banks and room for the radar and light AA that the war demanded, they fought in every Pacific campaign from Guadalcanal onward. They escorted carriers, led the night actions in the Solomons, bombarded beaches and stood radar picket off Okinawa, where the class took its heaviest losses to kamikazes. Nineteen were lost in the war. Dozens served on into the 1960s with the US Navy and for decades more with other navies.",
		"ships": "O'Bannon, Nicholas, Fletcher, De Haven, Chevalier, Strong, Johnston, Hoel, Heermann, Kidd, The Sullivans, Laffey, Charles Ausburne, Converse, Spence"},
	"mahan": {"real": "1,500 tons · 341 ft · 37 kn · 158 men (250 in war) · 5 x 5-inch/38, 12 x 21-inch tubes · 18 built 1936-37",
		"text": "The Mahans introduced the 5-inch/38 gun and high-pressure steam plant that became standard in US destroyers, and carried the heaviest torpedo battery of any American class: twelve tubes in three quadruple mounts. Pre-war they were the fleet's modern destroyers; by 1942 their open gun mounts and light AA showed their age. They fought at Pearl Harbor, in the Aleutians, the Solomons and the Philippines. Six were lost, including Mahan and Reid to kamikazes off Leyte and Ormoc Bay in December 1944.",
		"ships": "Mahan, Cummings, Drayton, Lamson, Flusser, Reid, Case, Conyngham, Cassin, Shaw, Tucker, Downes, Cushing, Perkins, Smith, Preston, Dunlap, Fanning"},
	"benson": {"real": "1,620-1,630 tons · 348 ft · 37.5 kn · 208-276 men · 5 x 5-inch/38 (4 in war), 10 x 21-inch tubes (5 in war) · 96 built 1939-43 (with the Gleaves class)",
		"text": "The Benson and Gleaves classes, nearly identical, were the destroyers the US Navy had in numbers when the war began. Their split engineering plant (alternating boiler and engine rooms) made them harder to disable with one hit. Early units gave up a gun and a torpedo bank for more AA. They were everywhere: Atlantic convoys, the Torch and Sicily landings, Guadalcanal, the Aleutians, and the fast carrier screens. Several were converted to high-speed minesweepers. About fifteen were lost in the two oceans.",
		"ships": "Benson, Mayo, Gleaves, Niblack, Laffey (DD-459), Woodworth, Monssen, Buchanan, Lansdowne, Lardner, McCalla, Aaron Ward, Duncan, Barton, Meredith"},
	"buckley": {"real": "1,400 tons · 306 ft · 23 kn · 213 men · 3 x 3-inch/50, 3 x 21-inch tubes, hedgehog, depth charges · 102 built 1943-44",
		"text": "Destroyer escorts were the answer to the U-boat: cheaper, slower ships with a heavy anti-submarine outfit and good AA, built in large numbers. The Buckleys were the first turbo-electric type and the most numerous. Most served in the Atlantic, but many went to the Pacific to screen convoys, escort carriers and amphibious groups and to hunt Japanese submarines; England (a Buckley) sank six in twelve days in May 1944, a record never matched. Off Samar in October 1944 the escorts of Taffy 3 charged a Japanese battle fleet.",
		"ships": "Buckley, England, Bull, Bunch, Reuben James, Samuel B. Roberts (Butler class, lost off Samar), Dennis, Raymond, John C. Butler"},
	"gato": {"real": "1,525 tons surfaced · 312 ft · 20.25 kn surfaced, 8.75 kn submerged · 60 men · 10 x 21-inch tubes (6 bow, 4 stern), 24 torpedoes, one 3-inch or 4-inch deck gun · 77 Gato, 120 Balao, 28 Tench built",
		"text": "The Gato and Balao classes were the fleet submarines that destroyed Japan's merchant marine. Long-ranged, air-conditioned, with radar from 1942, they patrolled from Pearl Harbor and Fremantle to the Japanese coast. US submarines, under two per cent of Navy personnel, sank about 55 per cent of all Japanese shipping lost in the war, some 1,300 ships including a battleship and eight carriers. The early war was crippled by the faulty Mark 14 torpedo (see Weapons). Fifty-two US submarines were lost with about 3,500 men. The Balao class had a thicker hull for a 400 ft test depth.",
		"ships": "Gato, Wahoo, Flasher, Barb, Silversides, Tang (Balao), Archerfish (Balao, sank Shinano), Harder, Growler, Trigger, Bowfin, Rasher, Sealion"},
	"pt_elco": {"real": "56 tons · 80 ft · 41 kn · 14 men · 4 x 21-inch torpedoes (Mk 8, later Mk 13 racks), 20 mm, .50 cal, later 40 mm · 326 Elco 80-footers built",
		"text": "Motor torpedo boats were a cheap way to fight in the confined waters of the Solomons and the Philippines. The Elco 80-footer was the largest US type, a plywood hull with three Packard petrol engines. PT boats harried the Tokyo Express off Guadalcanal, where PT-109 under John F. Kennedy was run down by the destroyer Amagiri, and in the Philippines they became barge-busters, their torpedoes replaced by guns. Their torpedo record against warships was poor; their value was in making Japanese night supply runs costly and in rescue and reconnaissance.",
		"ships": "PT-109, PT-59, PT-157, PT-105, Ron 3 (Philippines 1941-42)"},
	"atlanta": {"real": "6,000 tons · 541 ft · 32.5 kn · 673 men · 16 x 5-inch/38 in 8 twin mounts (12 in later units), 8 x 21-inch tubes · 8 built 1941-45",
		"text": "Designed as destroyer flotilla leaders and then rated as anti-aircraft cruisers, the Atlantas carried the heaviest dual-purpose battery afloat. They were too lightly protected for the surface fighting they found themselves in: at the Naval Battle of Guadalcanal on 13 November 1942, Atlanta was wrecked by Japanese fire and by friendly 8-inch shells from San Francisco, and Juneau was torpedoed and blew up with the loss of nearly all her crew, including the five Sullivan brothers. The later units served as what they were meant to be, AA ships in the fast carrier task forces.",
		"ships": "Atlanta, Juneau, San Diego, San Juan, Oakland, Reno, Flint, Tucson"},
	"brooklyn": {"real": "9,700 tons · 608 ft · 32.5 kn · 868 men · 15 x 6-inch/47 in 5 triple turrets, 8 x 5-inch/25 · 9 built 1937-39 (incl. St. Louis class)",
		"text": "The Brooklyns answered the Japanese Mogamis with fifteen rapid-firing 6-inch guns that could put out a volume of fire no other cruiser matched: ten rounds per gun per minute. Helena carried the first SG surface radar into the Solomons night actions and was sunk at Kula Gulf in July 1943 after being hit by three Long Lance torpedoes. Boise fought at Cape Esperance; St. Louis was torpedoed at Kolombangara and survived; Honolulu was torpedoed twice. None was lost except Helena. Several served postwar in South American navies into the 1990s.",
		"ships": "Brooklyn, Philadelphia, Savannah, Nashville, Phoenix, Boise, Honolulu, St. Louis, Helena"},
	"cleveland": {"real": "11,800 tons · 610 ft · 32.5 kn · 1,285 men · 12 x 6-inch/47 in 4 triple turrets, 12 x 5-inch/38 in twin mounts · 27 completed 1942-46, the largest cruiser class ever",
		"text": "A Brooklyn with one turret removed to make room for twin 5-inch mounts and far more light AA, the Clevelands became the standard light cruiser of the fast carrier task forces from 1943. Nine hulls were converted on the ways into Independence-class light carriers. They fought at Empress Augusta Bay, in the Marianas, at Leyte Gulf and off Okinawa, and bombarded islands from Tarawa to Japan. None was sunk, though several were badly damaged by kamikazes. Top-heavy with wartime additions, most were retired soon after 1945.",
		"ships": "Cleveland, Columbia, Montpelier, Denver, Santa Fe, Birmingham, Mobile, Vincennes, Pasadena, Springfield, Topeka, Biloxi, Houston, Miami, Astoria, Oklahoma City, Vicksburg, Duluth, Manchester"},
	"northampton": {"real": "9,050 tons · 600 ft · 32.5 kn · 1,200 men · 9 x 8-inch/55 in 3 triple turrets, 8 x 5-inch/25 · 6 built 1930-31",
		"text": "Treaty cruisers: ten thousand tons and 8-inch guns, with armour sacrificed to meet the limit. The Northamptons were the first US heavy cruisers with triple turrets. Houston was lost with the Asiatic Fleet at Sunda Strait on 1 March 1942, fighting alongside HMAS Perth against a Japanese invasion force. Chicago was torpedoed at Savo Island and sunk off Rennell Island in January 1943; Northampton was sunk by Long Lance torpedoes at Tassafaronga. Chester, Louisville and Augusta (Atlantic) survived the war.",
		"ships": "Northampton, Chester, Louisville, Chicago, Houston, Augusta"},
	"new_orleans": {"real": "10,100 tons · 588 ft · 32.7 kn · 870 men · 9 x 8-inch/55, 8 x 5-inch/25 · 7 built 1934-37",
		"text": "The last and best-protected of the US treaty cruisers, with a proper armoured belt and turrets. The class fought the hardest surface battles of 1942 and paid for it: Astoria, Quincy and Vincennes were sunk in forty minutes at Savo Island on 9 August 1942, the worst defeat in a fair fight in US Navy history. San Francisco was Admiral Callaghan's flagship at Guadalcanal on 13 November and was hit forty-five times. New Orleans and Minneapolis each lost their bows to torpedoes at Tassafaronga and were rebuilt. Tuscaloosa served in the Atlantic.",
		"ships": "New Orleans, Astoria, Minneapolis, Tuscaloosa, San Francisco, Quincy, Vincennes"},
	"baltimore": {"real": "14,500 tons · 673 ft · 33 kn · 1,700 men · 9 x 8-inch/55, 12 x 5-inch/38, 48 x 40 mm · 14 built 1943-46",
		"text": "Freed from the treaty, the Baltimores were what the US Navy had wanted all along: heavy cruisers with real armour, the superb 8-inch/55 Mark 12 gun with radar fire control, a dozen 5-inch dual-purpose guns and a mass of Bofors. They joined the fleet from 1943, screened the fast carriers through the Marianas, Leyte and Okinawa and bombarded Iwo Jima and the Japanese home islands. Canberra was torpedoed by aircraft off Formosa in October 1944 and towed 1,300 miles to safety. None was lost. Several later became the first guided-missile cruisers.",
		"ships": "Baltimore, Boston, Canberra, Quincy, Pittsburgh, St. Paul, Columbus, Helena, Bremerton, Fall River, Macon, Toledo, Los Angeles, Chicago"},
	"north_carolina": {"real": "37,500 tons · 729 ft · 28 kn · 1,880 men · 9 x 16-inch/45 in 3 triple turrets, 20 x 5-inch/38 · 2 built 1941",
		"text": "The first new American battleships in twenty years, designed under the treaty's 35,000-ton limit and armed with 16-inch guns when Japan refused to accept a 14-inch cap. North Carolina reached the Pacific in July 1942 and at the Eastern Solomons her AA fire helped break up the attack on Enterprise. She was torpedoed by I-19 in September 1942 (the same salvo sank Wasp) and repaired. Both ships spent the rest of the war with the fast carriers and bombarding islands, and both survived. North Carolina is preserved at Wilmington.",
		"ships": "North Carolina, Washington"},
	"south_dakota": {"real": "37,970 tons · 680 ft · 27.5 kn · 2,350 men · 9 x 16-inch/45, 16-20 x 5-inch/38 · 4 built 1942",
		"text": "A shorter, better-armoured version of North Carolina on the same tonnage. South Dakota and Washington fought the only battleship-against-battleship action of the Guadalcanal campaign on the night of 14-15 November 1942: South Dakota lost electrical power at the worst moment and was hit 26 times, while Washington's radar-directed 16-inch fire sank Kirishima. South Dakota's gunners also claimed 26 aircraft at Santa Cruz. Alabama and Massachusetts (which fought the French battleship Jean Bart at Casablanca) joined the Pacific fast carrier screens in 1943-44. All four survived.",
		"ships": "South Dakota, Indiana, Massachusetts, Alabama"},
	"iowa": {"real": "48,000 tons · 887 ft · 33 kn · 2,700 men · 9 x 16-inch/50, 20 x 5-inch/38, 80 x 40 mm · 4 completed 1943-44",
		"text": "The Iowas were built fast enough to run with the carriers, and their 16-inch/50 guns fired a 2,700 lb armour-piercing shell farther and harder than any gun but Yamato's. They joined the Pacific Fleet in 1944, screened the fast carriers, bombarded the Marshalls, Marianas, Iwo Jima, Okinawa and the Japanese coast, and shot down aircraft with the heaviest AA battery in the fleet. They never met the Japanese battle line: at Leyte Gulf, Halsey took them north after the decoy carriers. Japan surrendered on Missouri's deck in Tokyo Bay on 2 September 1945. All four served again in Korea, and were recommissioned in the 1980s.",
		"ships": "Iowa, New Jersey, Missouri, Wisconsin"},
	"texas": {"real": "27,000 tons · 573 ft · 21 kn (19 in war) · 1,800 men · 10 x 14-inch/45 in 5 twin turrets, 6 x 5-inch/51, 10 x 3-inch/50 AA · 2 built 1914 (New York class)",
		"text": "Texas is the last surviving dreadnought. Commissioned in 1914, she served with the British Grand Fleet in 1918, and was rebuilt with tripod masts in the 1920s. Too slow for the carrier task forces, she bombarded Casablanca, Omaha Beach, Cherbourg and southern France before coming to the Pacific to fire at Iwo Jima and Okinawa. Her sister New York did the same. In the game she stands for the old battle line of 1941: slow, tough and heavily armed, the ships that would have fought the Japanese fleet had the war come as both navies expected.",
		"ships": "Texas, New York (also the similar Nevada, Pennsylvania, Tennessee, Colorado classes)"},
	"yorktown": {"real": "19,800 tons · 809 ft · 32.5 kn · 2,200 men · about 90 aircraft · 8 x 5-inch/38 · 3 built 1937-41",
		"text": "Yorktown, Enterprise and Hornet were the carriers that held the Pacific in 1942. Hornet launched the Doolittle raid in April; Yorktown fought at Coral Sea and, repaired in three days at Pearl Harbor, at Midway, where her aircraft and Enterprise's sank four Japanese carriers before she was torpedoed by I-168 and lost. Hornet was sunk at Santa Cruz in October. Enterprise fought in nearly every major action of the war, was damaged repeatedly, and ended it as the most decorated US ship. The class could operate a large air group and was fast enough to work with the battle line.",
		"ships": "Yorktown (CV-5), Enterprise (CV-6), Hornet (CV-8)"},
	"essex": {"real": "27,100 tons · 872 ft · 33 kn · 3,400 men · 90-100 aircraft · 12 x 5-inch/38, up to 72 x 40 mm · 24 completed 1942-50",
		"text": "The Essex class was the backbone of the fast carrier force from 1943: big, fast, well protected, with a deck-edge elevator and the capacity for a hundred aircraft. From the Gilberts through the Marianas, Leyte Gulf, Iwo Jima and Okinawa the Essexes carried the Hellcats, Helldivers and Avengers that destroyed Japanese naval aviation. None was sunk, though Franklin and Bunker Hill were nearly lost to kamikazes and bombs in 1945 with over 1,100 dead between them. Several served into the 1970s, and Lexington until 1991.",
		"ships": "Essex, Yorktown (CV-10), Intrepid, Hornet (CV-12), Franklin, Ticonderoga, Randolph, Lexington (CV-16), Bunker Hill, Wasp (CV-18), Hancock, Bennington, Shangri-La, Bon Homme Richard"},
	"independence": {"real": "11,000 tons · 622 ft · 31 kn · 1,570 men · about 33 aircraft · 26 x 40 mm · 9 built 1943",
		"text": "In 1942 the Navy needed carriers quickly and converted nine Cleveland-class cruiser hulls on the building ways. The result was a small, fast carrier with a narrow deck and four small funnels on the starboard side, carrying a squadron of fighters and a squadron of Avengers. They let the fast carrier task groups form up in 1943 while the Essexes were still arriving. Princeton was bombed and lost at Leyte Gulf in October 1944, her magazine explosion also wrecking the cruiser Birmingham alongside. The others survived.",
		"ships": "Independence, Princeton, Belleau Wood, Cowpens, Monterey, Langley, Cabot, Bataan, San Jacinto"},
	"casablanca": {"real": "7,800 tons · 512 ft · 19 kn · 860 men · 27 aircraft · 1 x 5-inch/38, 16 x 40 mm · 50 built in one year, 1943-44",
		"text": "Henry Kaiser built fifty of these escort carriers in a single year on merchant-ship hulls. Slow and unarmoured, they covered the amphibious landings, flew anti-submarine patrols and ferried aircraft. Off Samar on 25 October 1944 the six escort carriers of Taffy 3 with their screen of destroyers and destroyer escorts were surprised by Kurita's battleships and heavy cruisers; Gambier Bay was sunk by gunfire and St. Lo by the first organised kamikaze attack, but the force's aircraft and the sacrifice of the screen turned the Japanese away. Liscome Bay was torpedoed off Makin, Ommaney Bay and Bismarck Sea lost to kamikazes.",
		"ships": "Casablanca, Liscome Bay, Gambier Bay, St. Lo, Kalinin Bay, Kitkun Bay, White Plains, Fanshaw Bay, Ommaney Bay, Bismarck Sea"},
	"liberty": {"real": "14,245 tons deadweight · 441 ft · 11 kn · 41 crew plus Armed Guard · 1 x 4-inch or 5-inch, 1 x 3-inch, 20 mm · 2,710 built 1941-45",
		"text": "The Liberty ship was the standard wartime cargo vessel, a simple British design built by welding in American yards in as little as a few weeks each. They carried the war across both oceans: tanks, fuel, food, ammunition and men. About 200 were lost to enemy action. In the Pacific, Liberties and the faster Victory ships supplied every landing from Guadalcanal to Okinawa and were the targets of Japanese submarines and aircraft in the forward areas.",
		"ships": "SS Patrick Henry (first), SS Jeremiah O'Brien and SS John W. Brown (preserved)"},
	# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Japan
	"kagero": {"real": "2,030 tons · 388 ft · 35 kn · 240 men · 6 x 12.7 cm/50 in 3 twin turrets, 8 x 61 cm tubes with 8 reloads (Type 93) · 19 built 1939-41",
		"text": "The Kageros were the finest destroyers of their day: fast, seaworthy, with six 12.7 cm guns in enclosed twin turrets and eight tubes for the 61 cm Type 93 oxygen torpedo, with a full set of reloads. They led the attacks on Pearl Harbor and Midway, ran the Tokyo Express to Guadalcanal and fought in nearly every surface action of the Solomons. Their torpedoes sank or crippled many Allied cruisers. Their weakness was AA and anti-submarine capability, and eighteen of the nineteen were lost; only Yukikaze survived the war, famous for her luck, and served in the Chinese navy until 1970.",
		"ships": "Kagero, Shiranui, Kuroshio, Oyashio, Hayashio, Natsushio, Hatsukaze, Yukikaze, Amatsukaze, Tokitsukaze, Urakaze, Isokaze, Hamakaze, Tanikaze, Nowaki, Arashi, Hagikaze, Maikaze, Akigumo (and the 20 Yugumo class)"},
	"fubuki": {"real": "1,750 tons (2,050 after rebuild) · 388 ft · 38 kn (34 rebuilt) · 219 men · 6 x 12.7 cm in 3 twin mounts, 9 x 61 cm tubes · 24 built 1928-33",
		"text": "When the Fubukis appeared in 1928 they outclassed every destroyer in the world and set the pattern for all later Japanese designs: six 12.7 cm guns in weatherproof twin mounts and nine torpedo tubes on a hull built for the Pacific. Stability problems forced a rebuild in the 1930s. By 1942 they were the workhorses of the destroyer squadrons, escorting the invasion convoys of early 1942 and fighting through the Solomons; Fubuki herself was sunk at Cape Esperance, Ayanami at Guadalcanal by Washington's secondary battery. Twenty-two of the twenty-four were lost.",
		"ships": "Fubuki, Shirayuki, Hatsuyuki, Shinonome, Usugumo, Shirakumo, Isonami, Uranami, Ayanami, Shikinami, Amagiri, Sagiri, Oboro, Akebono, Sazanami, Ushio, Akatsuki, Hibiki, Ikazuchi, Inazuma"},
	"akizuki": {"real": "2,700 tons · 440 ft · 33 kn · 300 men · 8 x 10 cm/65 Type 98 in 4 twin turrets, 4 x 61 cm tubes · 12 completed 1942-45",
		"text": "The Akizukis were designed as anti-aircraft escorts for the carriers, armed with the excellent long-barrelled 10 cm Type 98 gun, the best Japanese AA weapon of the war, in four twin turrets with a director. They were large, handsome ships, and torpedo tubes were added so that they could serve as ordinary destroyers too. They screened the carriers at Santa Cruz, the Philippine Sea and Leyte Gulf (where Akizuki was lost with Ozawa's decoy force) and escorted Yamato on her last sortie. Six were lost.",
		"ships": "Akizuki, Teruzuki, Suzutsuki, Hatsuzuki, Niizuki, Wakatsuki, Shimotsuki, Fuyutsuki, Harutsuki, Yoizuki, Natsuzuki, Hanazuki"},
	"nagara": {"real": "5,570 tons · 535 ft · 36 kn · 450 men · 7 x 14 cm/50 in single mounts, 8 x 61 cm tubes · 6 built 1922-25",
		"text": "The Nagaras and the similar Kuma and Sendai classes were the Japanese Navy's 5,500-ton cruisers: fast ships built in the 1920s to lead destroyer squadrons into the torpedo attack that was the heart of Japanese night-fighting doctrine. Twenty years on they were old, but they led the Tokyo Express and the Solomons night battles: Nagara led the destroyer screen at the Naval Battle of Guadalcanal, Jintsu (Sendai class) at Kolombangara where she was sunk, Sendai at Empress Augusta Bay where she was sunk. Most were lost to submarines and aircraft in 1944.",
		"ships": "Nagara, Isuzu, Natori, Yura, Kinu, Abukuma (also Jintsu, Sendai, Naka of the Sendai class)"},
	"myoko": {"real": "13,000 tons · 668 ft · 33.8 kn · 920 men · 10 x 20 cm/50 in 5 twin turrets, 8 x 12.7 cm AA, 16 x 61 cm tubes · 4 built 1928-29",
		"text": "Japan's first 10,000-ton treaty cruisers, and well over the limit when built, the Myokos carried ten 8-inch guns and a heavy torpedo battery. Haguro and Nachi fought at the Java Sea, where their torpedoes and gunfire destroyed the Allied ABDA squadron; Haguro also fought at Empress Augusta Bay and was sunk by British destroyers in the Malacca Strait in May 1945, the last surface action of the war. Nachi was sunk by aircraft in Manila Bay; Ashigara by the submarine Trenchant. Myoko, crippled at Leyte Gulf, was found at Singapore at the surrender.",
		"ships": "Myoko, Nachi, Ashigara, Haguro"},
	"takao": {"real": "13,400 tons · 668 ft · 34.2 kn · 920 men · 10 x 20 cm/50, 8 x 12.7 cm AA, 16 x 61 cm tubes · 4 built 1932",
		"text": "An improved Myoko with a massive bridge structure that gave the class its distinctive look. The Takaos were the heavy cruisers of the Guadalcanal campaign: Chokai led the force at Savo Island; Atago and Takao bombarded Henderson Field and fought at Guadalcanal on 14-15 November, where Kirishima was lost. At Leyte Gulf the class was ruined in a day: Atago and Maya were sunk by the submarines Darter and Dace on 23 October 1944, Takao crippled, and Chokai sunk off Samar two days later.",
		"ships": "Takao, Atago, Maya, Chokai"},
	"mogami": {"real": "13,670 tons · 661 ft · 35 kn · 850 men · 10 x 20 cm (built with 15 x 15.5 cm), 8 x 12.7 cm AA, 12 x 61 cm tubes · 4 built 1935-37",
		"text": "Built as light cruisers with fifteen 6.1-inch guns, the Mogamis were rearmed with ten 8-inch guns in 1939-40, which is what the game shows. Mikuma and Mogami collided while retiring from Midway and Mikuma was sunk by dive bombers; Mogami was rebuilt as an aircraft-carrying cruiser. Mogami and Kumano were at Savo Island and Guadalcanal. At Leyte Gulf Mogami was wrecked at Surigao Strait and Suzuya sunk off Samar; Kumano was sunk by aircraft in November 1944. Four 8-inch cruisers of real quality, all lost.",
		"ships": "Mogami, Mikuma, Suzuya, Kumano"},
	"tone": {"real": "15,200 tons · 661 ft · 35 kn · 870 men · 8 x 20 cm/50 in 4 twin turrets, all forward; 8 x 12.7 cm AA, 12 x 61 cm tubes, 5-6 floatplanes · 2 built 1938-39",
		"text": "The Tones carried all four 8-inch turrets forward so that the whole after deck could be given to floatplanes: they were the scouting cruisers of the carrier striking force. Tone's No. 4 floatplane was the late-launched scout at Midway whose delayed report helped seal the fate of Nagumo's carriers. Both ships fought at Pearl Harbor, Midway, the Eastern Solomons, Santa Cruz and Leyte Gulf, where Chikuma was sunk off Samar after helping to sink Gambier Bay. Tone was sunk at her moorings at Kure by carrier aircraft in July 1945.",
		"ships": "Tone, Chikuma"},
	"kongo": {"real": "32,000 tons (rebuilt) · 728 ft · 30 kn · 1,400 men · 8 x 35.6 cm/45 in 4 twin turrets, 14 x 15.2 cm, 8 x 12.7 cm AA · 4 built 1913-15, rebuilt 1930s",
		"text": "Kongo was built in Britain by Vickers as a battlecruiser in 1913; her three sisters were built in Japan. Rebuilt twice between the wars, they emerged as fast battleships, the only Japanese capital ships fast enough to escort the carriers, and so the hardest-worked. Kongo and Haruna bombarded Henderson Field on 13-14 October 1942, the most destructive naval bombardment the US Marines ever suffered; Hiei and Kirishima were lost in the two night battles of Guadalcanal in November, Hiei to aircraft after being crippled by destroyers and San Francisco, Kirishima to Washington's guns. Kongo was torpedoed by the submarine Sealion in November 1944; Haruna was sunk at Kure in July 1945.",
		"ships": "Kongo, Hiei, Kirishima, Haruna"},
	"fuso": {"real": "35,000 tons (rebuilt) · 698 ft · 24.5 kn · 1,900 men · 12 x 35.6 cm/45 in 6 twin turrets, 14 x 15.2 cm, 8 x 12.7 cm AA · 2 built 1915-17",
		"text": "Japan's first super-dreadnoughts, with twelve 14-inch guns in six turrets along the centreline. Rebuilt in the 1930s with a towering pagoda mast, the tallest in the fleet, they were too slow for the carrier war and spent most of it in home waters as training ships. In October 1944 they were sent through Surigao Strait as part of Nishimura's force: Fuso was torpedoed by destroyers and broke in two; Yamashiro fought on into the massed fire of six old US battleships (five of them raised from Pearl Harbor) and eight cruisers, the last battleship line ever to fire in anger, and sank with almost all hands.",
		"ships": "Fuso, Yamashiro"},
	"ise": {"real": "36,000 tons (rebuilt) · 708 ft · 25 kn · 1,400 men · 12 x 35.6 cm/45 (8 after 1943 conversion), 16 x 14 cm, 8 x 12.7 cm AA · 2 built 1917-18",
		"text": "Improved Fusos with a better turret arrangement. After Midway the two after turrets were removed and a flight deck for floatplane dive bombers built on the quarterdeck, making them hybrid battleship-carriers, though the aircraft were never embarked in action. In that form they served as part of Ozawa's decoy force at Leyte Gulf, where their furious AA fire and skilful handling brought them through hundreds of attacks almost unscathed. Both were sunk at Kure by carrier aircraft in July 1945, sitting on the bottom in shallow water.",
		"ships": "Ise, Hyuga"},
	"nagato": {"real": "39,000 tons (rebuilt) · 738 ft · 25 kn · 1,400 men · 8 x 41 cm/45 in 4 twin turrets, 18 x 14 cm, 8 x 12.7 cm AA · 2 built 1920-21",
		"text": "The first battleships in the world with 16-inch guns and the pride of the Japanese Navy between the wars; Nagato was Yamamoto's flagship when the order for Pearl Harbor was sent. Rebuilt in the 1930s with a single funnel and a pagoda mast. Mutsu blew up at anchor in June 1943 from a magazine explosion of unknown cause, with over a thousand dead. Nagato fought at the Philippine Sea and Leyte Gulf, where off Samar she fired her main battery at surface targets for the only time. The only Japanese battleship afloat at the surrender, she was expended at the Bikini atomic tests in 1946.",
		"ships": "Nagato, Mutsu"},
	"yamato": {"real": "65,000 tons standard, 72,800 full · 863 ft · 27 kn · 2,500-3,300 men · 9 x 46 cm/45 in 3 triple turrets, 12 (later 6) x 15.5 cm, 12-24 x 12.7 cm AA, up to 150 x 25 mm · 2 completed 1941-42 (Shinano completed as a carrier)",
		"text": "The largest battleships ever built, with 18.1-inch guns firing a 1,460 kg shell to 42 km, built in secret to outmatch anything the United States could pass through the Panama Canal. They spent most of the war at anchor at Truk, kept back for the decisive battle that never came in the form expected. Musashi was sunk by carrier aircraft in the Sibuyan Sea on 24 October 1944 after some 19 torpedo and 17 bomb hits; Yamato fired on the escort carriers off Samar the next day. On 7 April 1945 Yamato sailed for Okinawa on a one-way mission with fuel for a single passage and was sunk by about 380 carrier aircraft, taking some 3,000 men with her.",
		"ships": "Yamato, Musashi"},
	"akagi": {"real": "36,500 tons · 855 ft · 31 kn · 1,630 men · 66 aircraft (plus spares) · 6 x 20 cm, 12 x 12 cm AA · converted from a battlecruiser hull, completed 1927, rebuilt 1938",
		"text": "Akagi was laid down as a battlecruiser and converted to a carrier under the Washington Treaty, like the American Lexington and Saratoga. Rebuilt with a single full-length flight deck and a small island on the port side (an experiment not repeated), she was Nagumo's flagship for the attack on Pearl Harbor, the raids on Darwin and Ceylon, and Midway, where a single bomb from Enterprise's dive bombers among fuelled and armed aircraft on her hangar deck set her ablaze. She was scuttled by Japanese destroyers on 5 June 1942.",
		"ships": "Akagi"},
	"kaga": {"real": "38,200 tons · 812 ft · 28 kn · 1,700 men · 72 aircraft · 10 x 20 cm, 16 x 12.7 cm AA · converted from a battleship hull, completed 1928, rebuilt 1935",
		"text": "Kaga was a battleship hull saved from the scrapyard when the earthquake of 1923 wrecked the battlecruiser Amagi that was to have been converted. Slower than Akagi but carrying the largest air group in the striking force, she was at Pearl Harbor, Rabaul, Darwin and the Indian Ocean. At Midway on 4 June 1942 she was hit by four bombs from Enterprise's Dauntlesses within minutes of Akagi and Soryu, burned through the day and sank that evening with about 800 of her crew.",
		"ships": "Kaga"},
	"hiryu": {"real": "17,300 tons · 746 ft · 34 kn · 1,100 men · 57 aircraft (plus spares) · 12 x 12.7 cm AA · 1 built 1939 (with the similar Soryu, 1937)",
		"text": "Soryu and Hiryu were the first Japanese carriers designed as such from the keel: fast, lightly built, with large air groups. Hiryu's island was on the port side so that she could operate alongside Soryu without their traffic patterns crossing. Both were in Carrier Division 2 at Pearl Harbor, Wake, Darwin and Ceylon. At Midway Soryu was destroyed by Yorktown's dive bombers in the morning; Hiryu alone struck back and crippled Yorktown with two strikes before Enterprise's bombers found her that afternoon. Admiral Yamaguchi chose to go down with her.",
		"ships": "Hiryu (and Soryu)"},
	"shokaku": {"real": "25,700 tons · 845 ft · 34 kn · 1,660 men · 72 aircraft (plus spares) · 16 x 12.7 cm AA · 2 built 1941",
		"text": "The best carriers Japan built: big, fast, well protected for their type and with the largest air groups in the fleet. Shokaku and Zuikaku were the newest ships at Pearl Harbor and, being under repair and short of aircrew after Coral Sea (where Shokaku was bombed and Zuikaku's air group was cut down), they missed Midway, a turn of fate that may have decided the battle. They fought at the Eastern Solomons and Santa Cruz, where their aircraft sank Hornet. Shokaku was torpedoed by the submarine Cavalla at the Philippine Sea in June 1944; Zuikaku, the last Pearl Harbor carrier, was sunk as Ozawa's flagship and decoy at Cape Engano in October 1944.",
		"ships": "Shokaku, Zuikaku"},
	"zuiho": {"real": "11,260 tons · 672 ft · 28 kn · 785 men · 30 aircraft · 8 x 12.7 cm AA · 2 (Zuiho, Shoho) converted from submarine tenders 1940-41",
		"text": "Japan built several light carriers on hulls designed from the start for quick conversion: submarine tenders and seaplane carriers whose peacetime roles disguised the plan. Shoho was the first Japanese carrier lost in the war, sunk at Coral Sea on 7 May 1942 by aircraft from Lexington and Yorktown (\"Scratch one flattop\"). Zuiho was at Midway with the Aleutian force, had her deck holed at Santa Cruz, and screened the fleet at the Philippine Sea before being sunk with Ozawa's decoys at Cape Engano. The similar Chitose and Chiyoda were lost in the same action.",
		"ships": "Zuiho, Shoho (also Ryuho, Chitose, Chiyoda)"},
	"i_boat": {"real": "Type A1 (I-9 class): 2,919 tons surfaced · 373 ft · 23.5 kn surfaced, 8 kn submerged · 114 men · 6 x 53 cm bow tubes, 18 torpedoes, one 14 cm gun, one E14Y floatplane · 3 built 1941-42; the similar Type B1 (I-15 class), 20 built, was the most numerous",
		"text": "Japan's large cruiser submarines were built for the decisive fleet battle: fast on the surface, long-ranged, many carrying a small floatplane in a hangar forward of the conning tower for scouting. They scored notable successes against warships in 1942: I-19 sank the carrier Wasp and damaged North Carolina with one salvo; I-26 damaged Saratoga and sank Juneau; I-168 sank Yorktown at Midway; I-58 sank Indianapolis in 1945. But Japanese doctrine kept them hunting warships and running supplies to cut-off garrisons instead of attacking merchant shipping, and US escorts and radar destroyed them: of about 170 submarines, 127 were lost.",
		"ships": "I-15, I-17, I-19, I-21, I-25 (shelled Oregon and bombed its forests), I-26, I-27, I-29, I-168 (KD6), I-58 (B3)"},
	"subchaser": {"real": "No. 13 class: 438 tons · 167 ft · 16 kn · 68 men · 1 x 8 cm gun, 2 x 13 mm, 36 depth charges, hydrophones and sonar · about 60 built 1940-44",
		"text": "Japan entered the war with far too few escorts and never caught up. The No. 13 class subchasers, steel ships the size of a large trawler, were the most numerous purpose-built anti-submarine vessels of the Japanese Navy, convoying merchant ships and patrolling harbours. With a slow speed, a single small gun and a modest depth-charge outfit, they were outmatched by the US fleet submarines they hunted, though they and the later Kaibokan escorts sank a number of them. Most were lost to submarines and aircraft.",
		"ships": "No. 13 to No. 63 (even numbers in the class)"},
	"daihatsu": {"real": "Type A Daihatsu: 14 m (46 ft) · 8 kn · 70 troops or 10 tons cargo or one light tank · thousands built",
		"text": "The Daihatsu was the Japanese Navy's standard landing craft, a steel-hulled boat with a bow ramp that pre-dated the American Higgins boat. When destroyers became too costly to risk running supplies to Guadalcanal and New Guinea, the Japanese turned to barge convoys moving at night between islands and hiding under the trees by day. Hunting them became the main work of PT boats and destroyers in the Solomons in 1943. Some were fitted with field guns and extra automatic weapons as gun barges to fight the PTs off.",
		"ships": "Type A (14 m), Type D (17 m, with field guns or 25 mm)"},
	"daihatsu_gun": {"real": "Armed Daihatsu: 14-17 m · 8 kn · field gun or 37 mm plus machine guns and 25 mm", "text": "Gun barges were ordinary landing craft armoured with steel plate and armed with a field gun, a 37 mm or 25 mm cannon and machine guns to escort the supply barges and fight off PT boats. Slow and vulnerable in daylight, they were dangerous to the plywood PTs at close range at night.", "ships": ""},
	"cargo_maru": {"real": "Typical standard cargo ship: 6,000-7,000 gross tons · 12-14 kn · 50-60 crew · a 12 cm or 8 cm gun aft, machine guns",
		"text": "Japan began the war with about six million tons of merchant shipping, barely enough for a war economy that imported oil, iron, rice and nearly everything else from overseas. The Maru (the suffix carried by all Japanese merchant ships) were the lifeline of the empire and its garrisons. US submarines, and from 1944 carrier aircraft and mines, sank some 8.6 million tons and left Japan unable to feed its people or fuel its fleet by 1945. Convoys were small and poorly escorted until late in the war.",
		"ships": "Standard types 1A, 1B, 1C, 2A; thousands of named Maru"},
	"tanker_maru": {"real": "Standard tanker: 10,000 gross tons · 15-18 kn · a 12 cm gun, machine guns",
		"text": "Oil was the reason Japan went to war and the thing it could least afford to lose. Tankers carried crude from the conquered fields of Borneo and Sumatra to the refineries and the fleet. US submarines made them priority targets from 1943 and the fast, modern tankers were sunk faster than they could be built; by late 1944 the Combined Fleet was based at Lingga near Singapore simply to be close to the oil, and training was cut for lack of fuel. By 1945 almost nothing was getting through.",
		"ships": "Nippon Maru, Kokuyo Maru, Itsukushima Maru, and the 1TL/2TL standard tankers"},
}

## Battles of the Pacific war: id -> {title, date, text}
const BATTLES := [
	{"title": "Pearl Harbor", "date": "7 December 1941", "text": "Six Japanese carriers under Vice Admiral Nagumo launched 353 aircraft against the US Pacific Fleet at anchor on a Sunday morning. Arizona blew up and Oklahoma capsized; six more battleships were sunk or damaged, 188 aircraft destroyed and 2,403 Americans killed. The carriers were at sea and were missed, as were the fuel tanks and repair yards; all but two of the battleships were raised and returned to service. The attack brought the United States into the war with a unity the Japanese had not reckoned on."},
	{"title": "Battle of the Java Sea", "date": "27 February - 1 March 1942", "text": "A mixed American-British-Dutch-Australian squadron under the Dutch Rear Admiral Doorman tried to stop the invasion of Java. In a seven-hour running battle the Japanese cruisers Nachi and Haguro and their destroyers, with longer-ranged guns and the Type 93 torpedo, sank the Dutch cruisers De Ruyter and Java and three destroyers; Doorman went down with his flagship. Houston and Perth, escaping, ran into the invasion fleet in Sunda Strait on the night of 28 February and were sunk after a wild fight. Exeter and two destroyers were sunk the next day. The Allied naval presence in the East Indies was finished."},
	{"title": "The Doolittle Raid", "date": "18 April 1942", "text": "Sixteen Army B-25 bombers under Lieutenant Colonel James Doolittle flew off the carrier Hornet, escorted by Enterprise, 650 miles from Japan after the force was spotted by a picket boat. They bombed Tokyo and other cities and flew on to China, where most of the crews were rescued. The damage was slight, but the shock in Japan drove the Navy to commit to the Midway operation that cost it four carriers."},
	{"title": "Battle of the Coral Sea", "date": "4-8 May 1942", "text": "The first battle in which the opposing ships never saw each other. A Japanese force covering the invasion of Port Moresby was met by Lexington and Yorktown. The light carrier Shoho was sunk on 7 May; the next day Shokaku was bombed and Lexington was lost after torpedo and bomb hits caused fuel-vapour explosions, and Yorktown was damaged. A tactical draw that was a strategic Allied victory: the invasion was turned back and the two big Japanese carriers missed Midway."},
	{"title": "Battle of Midway", "date": "4-7 June 1942", "text": "Forewarned by code-breaking, Admiral Nimitz placed Enterprise, Hornet and the hastily repaired Yorktown north-east of Midway. On the morning of 4 June, after the torpedo squadrons had been destroyed almost to a man, dive bombers from Enterprise and Yorktown caught Akagi, Kaga and Soryu with armed and fuelled aircraft on their decks and set all three ablaze within six minutes. Hiryu's strikes crippled Yorktown (sunk by the submarine I-168 on 7 June) before she too was destroyed. Japan lost four fleet carriers, a heavy cruiser and most of its veteran aircrew. The war in the Pacific turned."},
	{"title": "Battle of Savo Island", "date": "9 August 1942", "text": "Two nights after the Marines landed on Guadalcanal, Vice Admiral Mikawa brought seven cruisers and a destroyer down the Slot and surprised the Allied screen off Savo Island. In under an hour of gunfire and torpedoes the heavy cruisers Astoria, Quincy, Vincennes and HMAS Canberra were sunk or left sinking and Chicago was damaged, for trivial Japanese losses. Mikawa withdrew without attacking the transports. It was the worst surface defeat in US Navy history, and the waters off Savo became Ironbottom Sound."},
	{"title": "Battle of the Eastern Solomons", "date": "24-25 August 1942", "text": "The second carrier battle of the campaign. Aircraft from Saratoga sank the light carrier Ryujo, sent out as bait; Shokaku's and Zuikaku's strike hit Enterprise with three bombs, which her crew survived with the help of North Carolina's AA fire. A Japanese troop convoy was turned back by aircraft from Henderson Field. A narrow American victory that kept the sea lanes to Guadalcanal open."},
	{"title": "Battle of Cape Esperance", "date": "11-12 October 1942", "text": "Rear Admiral Scott's cruisers and destroyers, using radar, crossed the T of a Japanese bombardment force under Rear Admiral Goto off Savo. The cruiser Furutaka and the destroyer Fubuki were sunk and Goto mortally wounded; the US destroyer Duncan was lost, partly to friendly fire. The first US night victory in the Solomons, though the confusion on both sides showed how much there was to learn."},
	{"title": "Bombardment of Henderson Field", "date": "13-14 October 1942", "text": "The battleships Kongo and Haruna fired some 900 14-inch shells into Henderson Field in eighty minutes, destroying half the aircraft on the field and most of its aviation fuel. Marines who survived it called it simply \"The Night\". It was the heaviest bombardment the field received and the Japanese army's best chance to retake the island, but the airfield was flying again within a day."},
	{"title": "Battle of the Santa Cruz Islands", "date": "26 October 1942", "text": "The fourth carrier battle. Japanese aircraft sank Hornet and damaged Enterprise, South Dakota and others; US aircraft damaged Shokaku and Zuiho. The Japanese won the exchange but lost about 100 aircraft and many irreplaceable veteran aircrew, and could not follow up. For two weeks Enterprise was the only US carrier in the Pacific."},
	{"title": "Naval Battle of Guadalcanal", "date": "12-15 November 1942", "text": "The decisive fight of the campaign. On the night of 12-13 November Rear Admiral Callaghan's cruisers and destroyers blundered into a bombardment force led by the battleships Hiei and Kirishima at point-blank range; Callaghan and Scott were both killed, Atlanta, Juneau and four destroyers were lost, but Hiei was crippled and sunk by aircraft the next day. On 14-15 November Rear Admiral Lee took Washington and South Dakota into the sound: South Dakota lost power and was mauled, but Washington's radar-directed fire destroyed Kirishima. The Japanese troop convoy was wrecked. Japan never again attempted to reinforce Guadalcanal in strength."},
	{"title": "Battle of Tassafaronga", "date": "30 November 1942", "text": "Rear Admiral Wright's five cruisers and six destroyers surprised Rear Admiral Tanaka's eight destroyers carrying supply drums to Guadalcanal. Tanaka lost one destroyer; his torpedoes sank Northampton, blew the bows off Minneapolis and New Orleans and tore open Pensacola. The last great success of the Long Lance and a lesson in how deadly Japanese destroyers remained when allowed to fire torpedoes before being engaged."},
	{"title": "Battle of the Bismarck Sea", "date": "2-4 March 1943", "text": "A convoy of eight transports and eight destroyers carrying 7,000 troops from Rabaul to Lae in New Guinea was destroyed by Allied land-based aircraft using skip-bombing and strafing. All eight transports and four destroyers were sunk. After this, Japan dared not send transports to New Guinea and relied on submarines and barges."},
	{"title": "Death of Yamamoto", "date": "18 April 1943", "text": "Code-breakers learned the itinerary of an inspection trip by Admiral Isoroku Yamamoto, commander of the Combined Fleet and planner of Pearl Harbor. Sixteen Army P-38 Lightnings flying from Guadalcanal at extreme range shot down his bomber over Bougainville. His successor Koga lacked his prestige and his gambler's touch."},
	{"title": "Battles of Kula Gulf and Kolombangara", "date": "6 and 13 July 1943", "text": "Two night actions in the central Solomons against the Tokyo Express. At Kula Gulf the light cruiser Helena was sunk by three Long Lance torpedoes after her flashless powder ran out and her gun flashes marked her; the Japanese lost two destroyers. At Kolombangara the Japanese cruiser Jintsu was destroyed by gunfire, but her destroyers' torpedoes sank the destroyer Gwin and damaged the cruisers Honolulu, St. Louis and HMNZS Leander. The Japanese were still better at night with torpedoes; the Americans were learning to use radar."},
	{"title": "Battle of Vella Gulf", "date": "6-7 August 1943", "text": "Commander Moosbrugger's six destroyers, operating without cruisers for the first time and using radar and a doctrine of torpedoes first, guns later, ambushed four Japanese destroyers. Three were sunk by torpedoes before they knew the Americans were there, for no US loss. The night-fighting advantage had changed hands."},
	{"title": "Battle of Empress Augusta Bay", "date": "2 November 1943", "text": "Rear Admiral Merrill's four Cleveland-class light cruisers and eight destroyers (Captain Arleigh Burke's Little Beavers) defended the Bougainville landing against a cruiser-destroyer force under Rear Admiral Omori. By radar-controlled gunnery at long range and constant manoeuvre to avoid torpedoes, Merrill sank the light cruiser Sendai and a destroyer and drove the Japanese off, losing no ships. The model surface action of the later war."},
	{"title": "Battle of Cape St. George", "date": "25 November 1943", "text": "Arleigh Burke's five destroyers intercepted five Japanese destroyers evacuating Buka. Torpedoes fired by radar sank two and gunfire a third, with no US damage. It was the last of the Solomons night battles and a perfect one; the Japanese never ran the Tokyo Express again."},
	{"title": "Tarawa", "date": "20-23 November 1943", "text": "The first assault on a fortified atoll in the Central Pacific drive. Marines wading across the reef under fire took Betio in 76 hours at a cost of about 1,000 dead; of 4,700 Japanese and Korean labourers, 17 Japanese and 129 Koreans survived. The escort carrier Liscome Bay was torpedoed off Makin with 644 dead. Tarawa taught the Navy and Marines the lessons in bombardment, amphibious tractors and underwater demolition that carried them to Okinawa."},
	{"title": "Battle of the Philippine Sea", "date": "19-20 June 1944", "text": "The largest carrier battle ever fought. Nine Japanese carriers under Ozawa attacked Spruance's Fifth Fleet, covering the invasion of Saipan, with four massive raids on 19 June; Hellcats and radar-directed AA destroyed some 350 aircraft in what the pilots called the Great Marianas Turkey Shoot, and the submarines Albacore and Cavalla sank Taiho and Shokaku. The next evening a strike at extreme range sank Hiyo. Japanese carrier aviation never recovered."},
	{"title": "Battle of Leyte Gulf", "date": "23-26 October 1944", "text": "The largest naval battle of the war, fought in four actions over three days as the Japanese fleet sortied against the Leyte landings. Submarines sank Atago and Maya and crippled Takao in the Palawan Passage; carrier aircraft sank Musashi in the Sibuyan Sea; Oldendorf's old battleships annihilated Nishimura's force at Surigao Strait in the last battleship line action in history; Halsey's carriers sank four of Ozawa's decoy carriers off Cape Engano; and Kurita's battleships and cruisers, having turned back, came through San Bernardino Strait to fall on the escort carriers of Taffy 3 off Samar, where the destroyers Johnston, Hoel and Samuel B. Roberts and the escort carriers' aircraft fought them off at the cost of their own lives. The first organised kamikaze attacks followed. Japan lost four carriers, three battleships, ten cruisers and eleven destroyers; the Imperial Navy ceased to exist as a fighting fleet."},
	{"title": "Battle off Samar", "date": "25 October 1944", "text": "Six escort carriers, three destroyers and four destroyer escorts of Taffy 3 under Rear Admiral Clifton Sprague were surprised at dawn by four battleships including Yamato, six heavy cruisers and eleven destroyers. The screen laid smoke and charged: Johnston, Hoel and Samuel B. Roberts were sunk, as were the carriers Gambier Bay and (to a kamikaze) St. Lo, but their torpedoes and the carriers' aircraft sank three heavy cruisers and so confused Kurita that he turned away from the invasion beaches. It remains the defining example of what destroyers were for."},
	{"title": "Iwo Jima", "date": "19 February - 26 March 1945", "text": "Three Marine divisions assaulted an eight-square-mile island of volcanic ash honeycombed with tunnels, after three days of naval bombardment that proved far too short. Nearly 7,000 Marines and sailors died and almost the whole garrison of 21,000. Off the island the escort carrier Bismarck Sea was sunk and Saratoga badly damaged by kamikazes. The flag raising on Mount Suribachi became the image of the Pacific war."},
	{"title": "Okinawa and Operation Ten-Go", "date": "1 April - 22 June 1945", "text": "The last and largest battle of the Pacific. While the Army and Marines fought 82 days ashore, the fleet offshore endured ten massed kamikaze attacks of about 1,900 aircraft: 36 ships were sunk and 368 damaged, and the Navy lost more men killed, nearly 5,000, than in any other battle of its history; the destroyers on the radar picket stations suffered worst. On 7 April the battleship Yamato, the cruiser Yahagi and four destroyers were sunk by carrier aircraft on a one-way sortie toward the island."},
	{"title": "The surrender in Tokyo Bay", "date": "2 September 1945", "text": "After the atomic bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki and the Soviet declaration of war, Japan accepted the Potsdam terms on 15 August. The instrument of surrender was signed aboard the battleship Missouri in Tokyo Bay, with the Allied fleet at anchor around her and a flight of 450 carrier aircraft overhead. The war had cost the US Navy some 62,000 dead in all theatres; the Imperial Japanese Navy lost some 300,000 men and nearly every major ship it had."},
]

## Aircraft: Data.AIRCRAFT key -> {real, text}
const AIRCRAFT := {
	"f4f": {"real": "Grumman F4F-4 Wildcat · 320 mph · 6 x .50 cal · 7,900 built", "text": "The US Navy's fighter of 1942. Slower and less agile than the Zero, it was tougher, with armour and self-sealing tanks, and in trained hands, using the Thach Weave and diving attacks, it held its own at Midway and Guadalcanal. Flown from escort carriers as the FM-2 to the end of the war."},
	"f6f": {"real": "Grumman F6F-5 Hellcat · 380 mph · 6 x .50 cal · 12,275 built", "text": "Designed with Wildcat experience and the power of the 2,000 hp R-2800 engine, the Hellcat outclimbed, outdove and outgunned the Zero and could take its punishment. From late 1943 it was the fighter of the fast carriers and accounted for about 5,200 Japanese aircraft, more than any other Allied naval fighter."},
	"sbd": {"real": "Douglas SBD-3/5 Dauntless · 255 mph · 1,000 lb bomb · 2 x .50 cal, 2 x .30 cal · 5,936 built", "text": "The Dauntless dive bomber won Midway: it sank all four Japanese carriers and the cruiser Mikuma, and later Ryujo and much else. Slow and obsolescent by 1943, it was accurate, steady in a dive and loved by its crews. It sank more Japanese shipping than any other US aircraft."},
	"sb2c": {"real": "Curtiss SB2C-3 Helldiver · 295 mph · 2,000 lb bombs in a bay · 2 x 20 mm · 7,140 built", "text": "The Dauntless's replacement was bigger and faster but troubled in development and disliked by its crews (\"Son of a Bitch 2nd Class\"). It flew from the fast carriers from late 1943 and helped sink Musashi, Yamato and the carriers at Leyte Gulf."},
	"tbf": {"real": "Grumman TBF/TBM Avenger · 275 mph · 1 x 22-inch Mk 13 torpedo or 2,000 lb bombs · 2 x .50 cal, 1 x .30 cal · 9,839 built", "text": "The standard torpedo bomber from Midway (where its combat debut cost five of six aircraft) to the end of the war. Big, rugged and versatile, it carried the improved Mark 13 torpedo that sank Musashi and Yamato, flew anti-submarine patrols from escort carriers and dropped bombs, rockets and mines."},
	"os2u": {"real": "Vought OS2U Kingfisher · 164 mph · floatplane scout · 1,519 built", "text": "The catapult floatplane carried by battleships and cruisers to spot gunfire and scout. Slow and vulnerable, Kingfishers also rescued downed airmen under fire, most famously picking up Eddie Rickenbacker's raft in 1942 and taxiing airmen out of Truk lagoon in 1944."},
	"a6m": {"real": "Mitsubishi A6M2 Zero (Type 0 carrier fighter) · 330 mph · 2 x 20 mm, 2 x 7.7 mm · 10,900 built (all marks)", "text": "In 1941-42 the Zero was the best carrier fighter in the world: light, long-ranged and extraordinarily agile, with heavy cannon. Its lightness came from leaving out armour and self-sealing tanks, and once Allied pilots learned not to dogfight it, and Hellcats and Corsairs arrived, its losses mounted. Japan could not replace it, and late in the war it carried bombs as a kamikaze."},
	"d3a": {"real": "Aichi D3A1 Val (Type 99 carrier bomber) · 240 mph · 250 kg bomb plus 2 x 60 kg · 2 x 7.7 mm, 1 x 7.7 mm rear · 1,495 built", "text": "The fixed-undercarriage Val was the dive bomber of Pearl Harbor and of 1942, when its crews were the best-trained in the world; it sank more Allied warships than any other Axis aircraft, including Hermes, Cornwall, Dorsetshire and many ships at Guadalcanal. By 1944 it was easy prey for Hellcats."},
	"b5n": {"real": "Nakajima B5N2 Kate (Type 97 carrier attack bomber) · 235 mph · 1 x Type 91 torpedo or 800 kg bomb · 1 x 7.7 mm · 1,150 built", "text": "The Kate's torpedoes sank or capsized the battleships at Pearl Harbor (its modified Type 91 could run in the shallow harbour) and sank Lexington, Yorktown and Hornet. Excellent in 1941, it was slow and unprotected and was replaced from 1944 by the B6N Jill."},
	"e13a": {"real": "Aichi E13A Jake (Type 0 reconnaissance seaplane) · 234 mph · 3-seat floatplane · 1,418 built", "text": "The long-ranged floatplane carried by Japanese cruisers and battleships for scouting. Tone's Jake found the US carriers at Midway, too late. Jakes flew patrols and night harassment raids throughout the war."},
}

## Weapons and equipment: a list of {title, text}
const WEAPONS := [
	{"title": "5-inch/38 gun and the Mark 37 director", "text": "The standard US destroyer and secondary battery gun, in single and twin mounts: a dual-purpose weapon equally at home against ships and aircraft, firing a 55 lb shell to about 18,000 yards at up to 15-20 rounds a minute per barrel with a good crew. Its power came from the Mark 37 director with radar ranging and, from 1943, the proximity (VT) fuze, which made it the deadliest AA gun of the war. In the game: the Fletchers' five mounts, the Atlantas' sixteen, and every modern US battleship's and carrier's secondary battery."},
	{"title": "Japanese 12.7 cm/50 Type 3 and 10 cm/65 Type 98", "text": "The 12.7 cm Type 3 in twin mounts armed every Japanese destroyer from the Fubukis on. A good surface gun with a heavier shell than the US 5-inch, it trained and elevated too slowly to be of much use against aircraft. The long 10 cm Type 98 of the Akizukis was far better, with a high muzzle velocity and 15 rounds a minute, but was never built in quantity."},
	{"title": "Cruiser guns: 6-inch/47, 8-inch/55 and 20 cm", "text": "The US 6-inch/47 of the Brooklyns and Clevelands fired a 130 lb shell at 8-10 rounds a minute, a volume of fire that could smother a destroyer or cruiser in a night action. The 8-inch/55 of the heavy cruisers fired a 335 lb (US Mark 21 super-heavy) shell to 30,000 yards. The Japanese 20 cm/50 Type 3 of the Myokos, Takaos, Mogamis and Tones was comparable, and Japanese cruisers added heavy torpedo batteries that US cruisers lacked. Armour matters: 6-inch shells bounce off a battleship, and even 8-inch fire does little against Yamato's belt."},
	{"title": "Battleship guns", "text": "US 14-inch/45 (Texas, Nevada, Pennsylvania), 16-inch/45 (North Carolina, South Dakota, Colorado) and 16-inch/50 (Iowa) fired armour-piercing shells of 1,500 to 2,700 lb; from 1942 the Mark 8 fire-control radar gave them accurate night and blind fire, which is how Washington killed Kirishima. Japan's 35.6 cm (Kongo, Fuso, Ise), 41 cm (Nagato) and the 46 cm of Yamato, firing a 1,460 kg shell to 42 km, relied on superb optics and were at a disadvantage at night and in poor visibility. Big guns train and reload slowly: a salvo every 30 seconds or so."},
	{"title": "Light AA: Bofors 40 mm and Oerlikon 20 mm; the Japanese 25 mm", "text": "The Swedish Bofors 40 mm, in twin and quadruple mounts with director control, and the Swiss Oerlikon 20 mm were fitted to US ships in ever greater numbers from 1942 and, with the 5-inch VT fuze, made the fast carrier force nearly impregnable to conventional air attack by 1944. The Japanese Type 96 25 mm was adequate in 1941 but its small magazines, slow training and poor sights could not stop Hellcats; Yamato carried up to 150 of them to no avail."},
	{"title": "US torpedoes: the Mark 14 and Mark 15 scandal", "text": "The 21-inch Mark 14 (submarines) and Mark 15 (destroyers) ran too deep, their magnetic exploders fired early or not at all, and their contact exploders failed on square hits. The Bureau of Ordnance blamed the crews for nearly two years. Submarine commanders tested the torpedoes themselves, and by late 1943 the faults were fixed; sinkings then soared. The Mark 15 could run 6,000 yards at 45 knots. In the game, US torpedoes have a dud chance that falls as the war goes on."},
	{"title": "The Type 93 Long Lance", "text": "The Japanese 61 cm Type 93 was powered by pure oxygen, which gave it a range of 20,000 m at 48 knots or 40,000 m at 36, a 490 kg warhead and almost no wake. No Allied torpedo came close, and the Allies did not even know of its performance until 1943. Fired in long-range spreads at the start of a night action, before the enemy knew the Japanese were there, it sank or crippled many Allied cruisers at Java Sea, Savo, Tassafaronga and Kula Gulf. Its oxygen flasks also made the ships carrying it dangerous when hit. The 53 cm Type 95 was the submarine version."},
	{"title": "Radar", "text": "The US Navy's SC and SK air-search sets and, above all, the SG surface-search radar (from mid-1942) with its plan-position scope let US ships see at night and in fog, and the Mark 3, 4 and 8 fire-control radars let the guns hit what the SG saw. It took a year to learn to use: at Savo and Tassafaronga radar information went unused or misread; by Vella Gulf and Empress Augusta Bay it decided battles. Japanese radar lagged two years behind and was never trusted; Japan relied on trained lookouts with giant binoculars, who were excellent, and on night-fighting drill."},
	{"title": "Sonar, depth charges and the hedgehog", "text": "Active sonar (the British ASDIC) could find a submerged submarine at a mile or two in good water, but lost contact as the ship passed over it, which was when the depth charges were dropped. The hedgehog, a forward-throwing spigot mortar firing 24 contact-fuzed bombs, fixed that: it fired while the target was still in the sonar beam and exploded only on a hit. England sank six Japanese submarines with it in May 1944. Japanese sonar and depth charges were poorer and their escorts too few, and US submarines dived deeper than Japanese depth charges were set for, a fact leaked by a congressman in 1943."},
	{"title": "Star shells, flashless powder and smoke", "text": "Night actions were fought by illumination: star shells burst behind the enemy to silhouette him, searchlights were used and quickly drew fire (Hiei lit her lights at Guadalcanal and was shot to pieces), and ships without flashless powder marked themselves with every salvo, as Helena did at Kula Gulf. Destroyers made smoke to hide themselves and their charges; Japanese destroyers laid smoke and turned away as they fired torpedoes, so that the Americans often did not know they had been fired on until the hits came."},
	{"title": "Damage control", "text": "US ships survived damage that sank Japanese ships because of training and design: compartmentation, fire-fighting foam and fog nozzles, portable pumps, trained repair parties and a doctrine of fighting the ship from undamaged positions. Franklin, hit by two bombs with a full deck of armed aircraft, came home; Taiho, hit by one torpedo, blew up from fuel vapour. In the game the damage-control rating of your crew and officers decides how fast fires go out and holes are plugged."},
]

## Game types that have no real-history entry (shore targets, rafts) are hidden from the list.
static func has_ship(type_id: String) -> bool:
	return SHIPS.has(type_id)
